// LiteralArgPromotion — **A BYTE RECORD HANDED TO A PARAMETER THE CALLEE WRITES, WHOSE PROVENANCE IS NOT
// ALREADY "A HEAP RECORD SOMEBODY OWNS", GETS ONE THE WRITE MAY LAND IN.**
//
// `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerStringLiteral` materialises a literal as the address of an IMMORTAL 56-byte
// `.rdata` record (`buffer@0` / `length@8` / `capacity@16 = ImmortalRecordCapacity`). `__str_append`'s
// copy-on-write detach (`MmRuntime.__mm_cow_detach`) already gets the BYTES half right — it sees the
// sentinel capacity, allocates a private buffer and copies into it — but it must then PUBLISH the new
// `buffer@0` / `capacity@16` / `length@8` back into the record, and a `.rdata` record is not writable:
//
//   * x64 / arm64 — the store faults. `grow("hello")` was `0xC0000005`, exit 3221225477, empty stdout.
//   * wasm32-wasi — linear memory has no read-only segment, so the identical store SUCCEEDS, and
//     because `GlobalDataTable` dedupes identical blobs the write is visible through every other use of
//     that literal. A wrong ANSWER, not a crash — which is why the pinning specs run on every target.
//
// ⭐ **THE PROGRAM IS LEGAL — the bootstrap oracle compiles it, prints `survived` and exits 0** — so
// refusing it is not available. The record simply has to be one a write may land in, and that is what
// this pass hands it: at every `call` / `tryCall` argument that fills a parameter the callee writes and
// cannot already be a heap record somebody owns, the argument is replaced by a fresh owned record and
// released by a `__str_decref` immediately after the call. There are TWO such arguments and they take two
// different promotions — a `stringLiteral` result (below) and a MERGE PHI (further below) — and every op
// this pass emits is an ordinary `MaxonOp.call`, so no op variant is added and no tier below learns a new
// shape.
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHY A REWRITE HERE AND NOT A DIFFERENT LOWERING FOR EVERY LITERAL.** The wider cure — lowering
// EVERY `stringLiteral` to a per-use HEAP record over the immortal blob, as `lowerConstArrayLiteral`
// already does for `b"…"` — makes every literal an owned temp and pulls in six ownership sites
// (`scanRuntimeUsage`'s `usesHeap` arm, the character-literal retype, the non-dominating match-pattern
// test block, `ModuleInit`'s drop-less synthesized functions, `emitManagedConstant`, interpolation
// fragments), and its sixth consequence CONTRADICTS THE RUNTIME FLOOR: every `print("hi")` program would
// carry the allocator. It is what the BOOTSTRAP does (its `main` `__mm_alloc`s a 48-byte record for the
// literal in `pick("ab", …)`, measured off `--emit-ir`), and shv2 declines it for that reason. This is the
// narrow cure: it costs one call on exactly the arguments that would otherwise fault or lose a write, and a
// program that hands no such argument to a writing parameter emits byte-identical code.
//
// ⚠ **THE COPYING PREDICATE IS "PROVABLY AN IMMORTAL RECORD", NOT "BORROWED", AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A
// WRONG ANSWER.** A blanket "COPY every borrowed byte-record argument" rule would rewrite `grow(t)` inside
// `outer(t String)` into a write on a COPY, and `outer`'s own caller — whose `var` really does own that
// record — would never see the append. Only a value the compiler can PROVE is `.rdata` may be COPIED, and
// a `stringLiteral` op's result is the one such value. The TWO-CALL-LEVEL case needs no
// second rule: `buildParamMutationSummary`'s least fixpoint puts the written bit on `outer`'s parameter
// because `grow` writes what it was handed, so the literal is promoted at `outer("hello")` — the frame
// that owns it — and `grow` writes the clone two frames down.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A MERGE PHI IS THE SECOND PROMOTION, AND IT IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION WITH A DIFFERENT ANSWER (W56).**
// `grow("a" if c else "b")` and `grow(try arr.get(5) otherwise "lit")` hand the callee a BLOCK ARG, not a
// `stringLiteral` result, and a phi is not provably ANYTHING: one edge of the second is a real heap element
// whose write MUST reach the array, the other is an immortal `.rdata` record that must be copied. A
// `__str_clone` here would lose the first write (MEASURED against the oracle, which prints `t=abXY` through
// exactly such a merge); the borrow the pass used to leave alone faults on the second (MEASURED
// 0xC0000005).
//
// ⇒ a block arg is promoted by `__str_retain`, which asks the RECORD — `capacity@16 == ImmortalRecordCapacity`
// — and clones or increfs accordingly. The release is the same `__str_decref` immediately after the call,
// and it balances both arms: the clone's one reference, or the incref's second one. **This is why the
// answer is a runtime entry and not a wider static rule**: no tier of this compiler can name a phi's
// provenance, because the two edges genuinely have different ones.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO ARMS ARE NOT ONE ARM SPELLED TWICE.** A provably-immortal argument keeps `__str_clone`
// because the answer is already known at compile time, and paying a load, a compare and a branch to
// re-derive it at run time would be measurement dressed as generality — and would move every golden the
// direct case already pins. The split is "is the provenance a compile-time fact?", and it has two honest
// answers.

// ⭐ **PROMOTE EVERY IMMORTAL-RECORD AND EVERY MERGED BYTE-RECORD ARGUMENT THAT FILLS A WRITTEN PARAMETER,
// WHOLE PROGRAM.**
//
// It runs at the tail of `semanticCheck` because that is the one pass holding BOTH halves of the
// question: the whole-program `ParamMutationSummary` — a least fixpoint over the call graph, and itself
// only correct once `resolveOverloadedCalls` has rewritten every overloaded callee name — and a Maxon
// module still one tier above lowering. It is a REWRITE inside a checking pass for the reason
// `resolveOverloadedCalls` is one: a rewrite keyed on a whole-program summary has nowhere earlier to
// stand, and rebuilding the summary in a pass of its own would be a second fixpoint over the same graph.
//
// ⚠ **THE OPS IT INSERTS ARE WHY `scanRuntimeUsage` MOVED BELOW THE PIPELINE** (see
// `Compiler.compileToCodeResult`). A `__str_clone` or `__str_retain` appended after the usage scan had
// already run would be a call to a runtime entry nothing installed — an unresolved relocation, not a leak.
//
// ⚠ **A PROGRAM THAT HAS ALREADY REPORTED AN ERROR IS LEFT ALONE.** `PassPipeline.run` throws on the
// diagnostics this pass's own file has just filed, so the module is about to be discarded; rewriting it
// could only risk a panic on malformed input in front of the diagnostic that explains it.
export function promoteArgsNeedingAWritableRecord(project Project, module MaxonModule, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary)
	if projectHasErrors(project) 'alreadyRefused'
		return
	end 'alreadyRefused'

	for i in 0 upto module.functions.count() 'eachFunc'
		let func = functionAt(module, index: i)
		// The same reachability gate `scanRuntimeUsage` and `lowerMaxonToStd` apply: a stdlib body no back
		// end will receive is not worth rewriting, and an op inserted into one would sit in front of a scan
		// that deliberately skips the function.
		if project.stdlibFacts.unreachable.contains(func.name) 'notInTheProgram'
			continue
		end 'notInTheProgram'

		promoteInFunction(project, module: module, func: func, funcIndex: i, mutationSummary: mutationSummary)
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'promoteArgsNeedingAWritableRecord'

// ⭐⭐ **THE FUNCTION RECORD AS A REFERENCE THIS FRAME OWNS, NOT AS A BORROW OF THE MODULE.** Reading an
// element out of `module.functions` and BINDING it makes the binding a borrow of the whole module for as
// long as it lives (E3070) — and the promotion below writes `module.ops` throughout, which is precisely
// what the rule forbids holding a borrow across. Returning it takes the reference instead: shv2's return
// convention promotes a BORROWED managed value to an owned one (`Parser.emitOwnedValueReturn` — *"a
// borrowed struct/union/instance INCREFS"*), so the caller holds a `+1` that no write to the module can
// invalidate. That is the language's answer to "I need to keep this element", and it costs one incref per
// function of the program.
function functionAt(module MaxonModule, index FuncIndex) returns IrFunction
	return try module.functions.get(index) otherwise panic("functionAt: module.functions.get OOB at {index}")
end 'functionAt'

// One function, in three walks — and every function in almost every program leaves after the first,
// having allocated nothing.
//
// ⭐ **THE GATE IS "DOES THIS BODY CALL ANYTHING THAT WRITES A PARAMETER?", AND IT IS WHAT KEEPS THIS
// FREE.** It is asked first and on its own, before any storage is claimed, because both other orders make
// a compile that changes nothing pay for it: seeding the provenance columns first would allocate two dense
// columns for every `print("hello")` in the program, and folding the two walks together would allocate them
// before knowing whether anything can consume them.
function promoteInFunction(project Project, module MaxonModule, func IrFunction, funcIndex FuncIndex, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary)
	if not callsAWritingCallee(module, func: func, mutationSummary: mutationSummary) 'writesNothingItWasHanded'
		return
	end 'writesNothingItWasHanded'

	let provenance = ArgProvenance.of(module, func: func)
	var minter = PromotedValueMinter.over(func)
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		promoteInBlock(project, module: module, block: try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("promoteInFunction: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks"), func: func, funcIndex: funcIndex, provenance: provenance, minter: minter, mutationSummary: mutationSummary)
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'promoteInFunction'

// The gate walk. Only a callee with a non-empty written-parameter mask can give an argument a reason to
// be promoted, so a body that calls none is finished here.
function callsAWritingCallee(module MaxonModule, func IrFunction, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary) returns bool
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("callsAWritingCallee: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			if opCallsAWritingCallee(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("callsAWritingCallee: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops"), mutationSummary: mutationSummary) 'found'
				return true
			end 'found'
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachBlock'
	return false
end 'callsAWritingCallee'

// The per-op arm of the gate. `collectCallParamEdgesAt`'s arm list, asked of the same two variants for the
// same reason — its `asyncCall` exclusion included: shv2 refuses a managed `async` argument outright
// (`requireAsyncScalarParams`), so no spawn can carry a String record at all. The non-call bands range to
// `false`, so a newly appended op fails to compile here rather than silently dropping out of the gate
// (the band-append invariant).
function opCallsAWritingCallee(op MaxonOp, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary) returns bool
	return match op 'op'
		call(_, callee, _, _, _, _, _, _) gives mutationSummary.maskOf(callee) != NoParamPositions
		literal to stringLiteral gives false
		tryCall(_, _, callee, _, _, _, _, _, _) gives mutationSummary.maskOf(callee) != NoParamPositions
		throwError to indirectCall gives false
		asyncCall gives false
		await to tryAwait gives false
		sizeofType gives false
		constArrayLiteral gives false
		switch gives false
		witnessDispatch to witnessTryDispatch gives false
		panicAbortValue gives false
		// The error-flag encode calls nothing, so it writes through no parameter.
		errorFlagEncode gives false
		// ⭐ **A SEND'S ARGUMENTS ARE ALREADY WRITABLE BY THE TIME THIS PASS COULD ACT, AND THAT IS WHY BOTH
		// ANSWER `false`.** This pass exists to give an IMMORTAL `.rdata` record to a callee that writes its
		// parameter; a message's arguments went into the request box through `Parser.moveIntoGreenThread`,
		// which PROMOTES a borrowed byte record to a fresh owned copy — so nothing immortal survives to the
		// handler. A `serviceSpawn`'s only operand is the factory's own fresh box.
		serviceSpawn to serviceReplyCell gives false
	end 'op'
end 'opCallsAWritingCallee'

// ⭐ **WHICH PROMOTION AN ARGUMENT AT A WRITTEN POSITION NEEDS** — the question this pass exists to answer,
// as a value rather than as two booleans a caller could read in the wrong order.
enum WritableRecordPromotion
	// Neither an immortal record nor a merge, so the callee already has a record it may write.
	none
	// A `stringLiteral` result: the provenance is a COMPILE-TIME fact, so the copy is emitted directly.
	copyImmortalRecord
	// A merge phi: the provenance differs BY EDGE, so it is decided at run time off the record itself.
	retainMergedRecord
end 'WritableRecordPromotion'

// The runtime entry each promotion spends. `none` has none, and asking for it is a caller that skipped its
// own gate rather than a case to answer with an empty name.
function promotionCallee(promotion WritableRecordPromotion) returns ByteArray
	return match promotion 'which'
		none panic("promotionCallee: the `none` promotion names no runtime entry — every caller gates on `promotionAtWrittenPosition` answering otherwise")
		copyImmortalRecord gives StrCloneName
		retainMergedRecord gives StrRetainName
	end 'which'
end 'promotionCallee'

// ⭐ **THE TWO PROVENANCE COLUMNS ONE FUNCTION'S ARGUMENTS ARE TESTED AGAINST**, dense by ValueId so the
// per-argument question below is two array reads.
//
// They are one type rather than two locals because they answer ONE question between them, through the one
// `promotionFor` reader, and that answer is asked by TWO callers — the cheap whole-call gate and the plan
// loop that acts on it. Two loose columns would let those two ask the pair in different orders and reach
// different answers, which is the same drift `anyArgNeedsAWritableRecord` already exists to prevent.
type ArgProvenance
	// The address of an immortal `.rdata` record. A `stringLiteral` op is the only producer, and that is a
	// claim about every other route rather than an omission: an interpolation, a `__str_clone`, a `var`
	// String's promotion at its declaration and a `returns String` hand-off all ALLOCATE, and everything
	// else is a borrow of something that did.
	var isImmortal as BoolArray
	// A merge phi of BYTE-RECORD type — the value whose provenance is not a compile-time fact at all,
	// because its edges may disagree. The TAG is part of the test: a merge of two `int`s owns no record and
	// a merge of two arrays cannot be immortal (every array record is `__mm_alloc`'d), so neither needs a
	// writable record and neither may be handed to `__str_retain`, which reads a String record's fields.
	var isMergedByteRecord as BoolArray

	static function of(module MaxonModule, func IrFunction) returns ArgProvenance
		var provenance = Self{isImmortal: BoolArray.create(), isMergedByteRecord: BoolArray.create()}
		provenance.isImmortal.resize(func.valueTags.count())
		provenance.isMergedByteRecord.resize(func.valueTags.count())

		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("ArgProvenance.of: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			provenance.notePhis(block, func: func)
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				provenance.noteOp(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("ArgProvenance.of: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops"), func: func)
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
		return provenance
	end 'of'

	// A block's own phis. `IrBlock.blockArgs` is the parser's on-the-fly SSA record and is the exact set;
	// deriving "defined by no op" instead would also admit a PARAMETER, which must not be promoted here.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND A PARAMETER NEEDS NO PROMOTION, BY THE TWO ROUTES IT CAN REACH A WRITE.** Handed to a written
	// slot DIRECTLY (`outer(t String)` doing `grow(t)`), `addCallParamEdges` records the edge off exactly that
	// "the argument IS one of my parameters" test, so the fixpoint marks `outer`'s own slot and every call
	// site's literal was already promoted one frame up — the record is heap by the time the callee sees it.
	// Reaching a written slot through a MERGE, the argument at that slot is the PHI and not the parameter, so
	// it is promoted below and the parameter is never the value substituted. Promoting parameters too would
	// re-copy the very borrow the direct case is careful to pass through untouched.
	function notePhis(block IrBlock, func IrFunction)
		for arg in block.blockArgs 'eachPhi'
			if not tagIsByteRecord(try func.valueTags.get(arg.valueId) otherwise panic("ArgProvenance.notePhis: phi v{arg.valueId} of '{String.from(func.name)}' has no recorded type — every SSA value is minted through Parser.mintValue")) 'ownsNoByteRecord'
				continue
			end 'ownsNoByteRecord'

			try self.isMergedByteRecord.set(arg.valueId, value: true) otherwise panic("ArgProvenance.notePhis: phi v{arg.valueId} of '{String.from(func.name)}' is outside its own value space of {func.valueTags.count()} — every SSA value is minted through Parser.mintValue")
		end 'eachPhi'
	end 'notePhis'

	// The non-`stringLiteral` bands range to `break`, so a newly appended op fails to compile here rather
	// than silently dropping out of the provenance walk (the band-append invariant).
	function noteOp(op MaxonOp, func IrFunction)
		match op 'op'
			call then break 'op'
			literal to storeIndirect then break 'op'
			stringLiteral(result, _) then try self.isImmortal.set(result, value: true) otherwise panic("ArgProvenance.noteOp: v{result} of '{String.from(func.name)}' is outside its own value space of {func.valueTags.count()} — every SSA value is minted through Parser.mintValue")
			tryCall to indirectCall then break 'op'
			asyncCall to tryAwait then break 'op'
			sizeofType then break 'op'
			constArrayLiteral then break 'op'
			switch then break 'op'
			witnessDispatch to witnessTryDispatch then break 'op'
			panicAbortValue then break 'op'
			// The error-flag encode takes no ARGUMENT position — its operand is an enum value the parser already
			// materialized, never a literal this pass could promote.
			errorFlagEncode then break 'op'
			// Neither service op DEFINES a byte record: a spawn's result is a mailbox pointer and a send
			// defines nothing at all.
			serviceSpawn to serviceReplyCell then break 'op'
		end 'op'
	end 'noteOp'

	// Which promotion `value` needs, read off the two columns. A ValueId past their end is one this pass
	// itself minted — a promotion's result, or a release's `void` — which is by construction neither, so the
	// short read IS the answer.
	function promotionFor(value ValueId) returns WritableRecordPromotion
		if (try self.isImmortal.get(value) otherwise false) 'immortalRecord'
			return WritableRecordPromotion.copyImmortalRecord
		end 'immortalRecord'

		if (try self.isMergedByteRecord.get(value) otherwise false) 'mergePhi'
			return WritableRecordPromotion.retainMergedRecord
		end 'mergePhi'

		return WritableRecordPromotion.none
	end 'promotionFor'
end 'ArgProvenance'

// Rebuild one block's `opRefs`, splicing a promotion in front of every call that hands an immortal record
// or a merge to a written parameter and a `__str_decref` behind it. A block with no such call keeps the
// array it already had, so no block of a function this pass does not change is rewritten.
//
// ⚠ **`opRefs` IS REBUILT AND FIELD-ASSIGNED, and the call op is REPLACED through `module.ops.set`
// rather than edited — both forced by the PARSE MEMO.** `IrModule.merge` copies op REFERENCES into the
// merged module (a Maxon op is a heap box), so an argument list edited in place would be the file
// artifact's own list and would corrupt every later compile of the project; `opRefs`, by contrast, is
// rebuilt fresh by `relocateIrBlock`, so this block's belongs to the merged module alone. It is the same
// split `IrBlock`'s header states for the phi model.
function promoteInBlock(project Project, module MaxonModule, block IrBlock, func IrFunction, funcIndex FuncIndex, provenance ArgProvenance, minter PromotedValueMinter, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary)
	var newRefs = OpIndexArray.create()
	var promotedAny = false
	for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
		promotedAny = promoteCallAt(project, module: module, opIdx: opIdx, func: func, funcIndex: funcIndex, provenance: provenance, minter: minter, mutationSummary: mutationSummary, newRefs: newRefs) or promotedAny
	end 'eachOp'

	if not promotedAny 'unchanged'
		return
	end 'unchanged'

	block.opRefs = newRefs
end 'promoteInBlock'

// The per-op arm of the rewrite walk. Appends `opIdx` to `newRefs` — preceded by its promotions and followed
// by its releases when this op is a call that needs them — and answers whether anything was spliced in.
// Same arm list, same band discipline and same `asyncCall` exclusion as the gate above.
function promoteCallAt(project Project, module MaxonModule, opIdx OpIndex, func IrFunction, funcIndex FuncIndex, provenance ArgProvenance, minter PromotedValueMinter, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary, newRefs OpIndexArray) returns bool
	let op = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("promoteCallAt: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops")
	match op 'op'
		call(_, callee, args, argLabels, _, _, _, _) then return promoteArgsOf(project, module: module, opIdx: opIdx, func: func, funcIndex: funcIndex, callee: callee, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, provenance: provenance, minter: minter, mutationSummary: mutationSummary, newRefs: newRefs)
		literal to stringLiteral then break 'op'
		tryCall(_, _, callee, args, argLabels, _, _, _, _) then return promoteArgsOf(project, module: module, opIdx: opIdx, func: func, funcIndex: funcIndex, callee: callee, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, provenance: provenance, minter: minter, mutationSummary: mutationSummary, newRefs: newRefs)
		throwError to indirectCall then break 'op'
		asyncCall then break 'op'
		await to tryAwait then break 'op'
		sizeofType then break 'op'
		constArrayLiteral then break 'op'
		switch then break 'op'
		witnessDispatch to witnessTryDispatch then break 'op'
		panicAbortValue then break 'op'
		// Same: no argument position, so no promotion decision to record for it.
		errorFlagEncode then break 'op'
		// See `opCallsAWritingCallee`: a message's arguments are owned records by the time they reach the
		// request box, so there is no immortal one here to promote.
		serviceSpawn to serviceReplyCell then break 'op'
	end 'op'

	newRefs.push(opIdx)
	return false
end 'promoteCallAt'

// The shared body of the two call arms: decide which argument positions need a writable record, emit the
// promotions ahead of the call, rewrite the op's argument list, and emit the releases behind it.
//
// A callee with NO signature is left alone for `addCallParamEdges`' reason — it is compiler-internal
// (`__managed_append` and friends, whose receiver writes the parser recorded directly) or undeclared, which
// is E3004 and so cannot reach a pass that runs only on an error-free project.
// ⚠ **IT REPLACES THE OP AT `opIdx`, SO IT DOES NOT TAKE THE OP.** The store releases the record, and a
// caller that had handed its own borrow in would be left holding a released pointer — E3070's shape, and the
// reason this door reads the op back for itself at the one line that needs it.
function promoteArgsOf(project Project, module MaxonModule, opIdx OpIndex, func IrFunction, funcIndex FuncIndex, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, argLabels ByteArrayArray, provenance ArgProvenance, minter PromotedValueMinter, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary, newRefs OpIndexArray) returns bool
	let mask = mutationSummary.maskOf(callee)
	// Short-circuited, so the second lookup is paid only by the handful of calls whose callee writes
	// anything at all.
	if mask == NoParamPositions or not project.funcSignatures.contains(callee) 'noWrittenPositionToFill'
		newRefs.push(opIdx)
		return false
	end 'noWrittenPositionToFill'

	let sig = try project.funcSignatures.get(callee) otherwise panic("promoteArgsOf: funcSignatures has no entry for '{String.from(callee)}' one line after `contains` answered true for it")
	// Asked before anything is built, because a call to a writing callee is common and a literal or a merge
	// AT one of its written positions is not: without it every such call would claim four arrays to discover
	// it had nothing to put in them.
	if not anyArgNeedsAWritableRecord(sig, mask: mask, args: args, argLabels: argLabels, provenance: provenance) 'nothingToPromote'
		newRefs.push(opIdx)
		return false
	end 'nothingToPromote'

	var promotedArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
	var promoted = ValueIdArray.create()
	var sources = ValueIdArray.create()
	var callees = ByteArrayArray.create()
	for i in 0 upto args.count() 'eachArg'
		let argId = try args.get(i) otherwise panic("promoteArgsOf: args.get OOB at {i}")
		let promotion = promotionAtWrittenPosition(sig, mask: mask, argIndex: i, argId: argId, argLabels: argLabels, provenance: provenance)
		if promotion == WritableRecordPromotion.none 'keepArgument'
			promotedArgs.push(argId)
			continue
		end 'keepArgument'

		let writable = minter.mint(try func.valueTags.get(argId) otherwise panic("promoteArgsOf: value v{argId} of '{String.from(func.name)}' has no recorded type — every SSA value is minted through Parser.mintValue"))
		promotedArgs.push(writable)
		promoted.push(writable)
		sources.push(argId)
		callees.push(promotionCallee(promotion))
	end 'eachArg'

	// The promotion and the release are the compiler's own ops, so they ride the span of the statement that
	// made them necessary — `emitRuntimeCall`'s convention for every synthesized retain and release.
	let range = project.opRanges.get(opIdx)

	// Promotions first, in argument order — each must dominate the use it feeds; then the call; then the
	// releases. For a `tryCall` the releases still land among this block's BODY ops, i.e. ahead of the
	// error-flag branch the parser emits as the terminator, so they run whichever way the call left.
	//
	// ⭐ **ONE RELEASE SERVES BOTH PROMOTIONS, AND THAT IS THE PROPERTY THAT MAKES THEM ONE MECHANISM.**
	// `__str_clone` hands back a record whose sole reference is this one; `__str_retain` hands back either
	// such a record or a SECOND reference to a live one. `__str_decref` is the balance of both — it frees
	// only at the last owner (`emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner`), so the co-owned arm decrements and leaves the
	// borrow's own owner untouched.
	for i in 0 upto promoted.count() 'eachPromotion'
		newRefs.push(appendPromotionRuntimeCall(module, project: project, funcIndex: funcIndex, result: try promoted.get(i) otherwise panic("promoteArgsOf: promoted.get OOB at {i}"), callee: try callees.get(i) otherwise panic("promoteArgsOf: callees.get OOB at {i} — the three columns are pushed together"), arg: try sources.get(i) otherwise panic("promoteArgsOf: sources.get OOB at {i} — the three columns are pushed together"), range: range))
	end 'eachPromotion'

	let original = try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("promoteArgsOf: module.ops.get OOB at {opIdx}")
	let rewritten = withRewrittenArgs(original, args: promotedArgs)
	try module.ops.set(opIdx, value: rewritten) otherwise panic("promoteArgsOf: module.ops.set OOB at {opIdx}")
	newRefs.push(opIdx)

	for writable in promoted 'eachRelease'
		newRefs.push(appendPromotionRuntimeCall(module, project: project, funcIndex: funcIndex, result: minter.mint(ValueTypeTag.void), callee: StrDecrefName, arg: writable, range: range))
	end 'eachRelease'

	return true
end 'promoteArgsOf'

// Does ANY of this call's arguments need a writable record? The plan loop's own predicate, asked over the
// whole argument list first — one question, two askers, so the cheap gate cannot drift from what it gates.
function anyArgNeedsAWritableRecord(sig FuncSignature, mask ParamPositionMask, args ValueIdArray, argLabels ByteArrayArray, provenance ArgProvenance) returns bool
	for i in 0 upto args.count() 'eachArg'
		if promotionAtWrittenPosition(sig, mask: mask, argIndex: i, argId: try args.get(i) otherwise panic("anyArgNeedsAWritableRecord: args.get OOB at {i}"), argLabels: argLabels, provenance: provenance) != WritableRecordPromotion.none 'needsOne'
			return true
		end 'needsOne'
	end 'eachArg'
	return false
end 'anyArgNeedsAWritableRecord'

// Which promotion source argument `argIndex` needs, given that it must fill a parameter the callee WRITES —
// `none` when it fills any other position, whatever its own provenance is.
//
// ⚠ **IT READS THE SOURCE-ORDER COLUMNS AND BRIDGES THEM WITH `argSlotPosition`, exactly as
// `checkImmutableArgToMutatingParam` must** — the value is a fact about the argument the SOURCE wrote,
// while the mask is indexed by DECLARATION position, and `grow(1, dest: "x")` writes parameter 1 with the
// offending argument at source position 1 only by coincidence. Inverting the permutation by hand would be
// a second slotting.
function promotionAtWrittenPosition(sig FuncSignature, mask ParamPositionMask, argIndex ParamCount, argId ValueId, argLabels ByteArrayArray, provenance ArgProvenance) returns WritableRecordPromotion
	let promotion = provenance.promotionFor(argId)
	if promotion == WritableRecordPromotion.none 'alreadyWritable'
		return promotion
	end 'alreadyWritable'

	let pos = argSlotPosition(sig.paramNames, argIndex: argIndex, label: try argLabels.get(argIndex) otherwise panic("promotionAtWrittenPosition: argLabels.get OOB at {argIndex} — a call's argument columns are parallel by construction"))
	if pos >= sig.paramCount() or not maskHasParam(mask, param: pos) 'notAWrittenPosition'
		return WritableRecordPromotion.none
	end 'notAWrittenPosition'

	return promotion
end 'promotionAtWrittenPosition'

// Append a one-argument runtime call to the module and return the index it landed at, recording its span
// and the origin of the value it defines in the SAME lockstep `FileParseArtifact.emitOp` keeps: the span
// table asserts it is appended in step with `module.ops`, so an op that skipped it would misattribute
// every later span by one.
//
// It appends to `module.ops` and hands the index back rather than going through `IrModule.appendOp`,
// because the caller SPLICES the index into a rebuilt `opRefs` at a chosen position — where `appendOp`
// would push it onto the block's tail, which is past the call it must precede.
function appendPromotionRuntimeCall(module MaxonModule, project Project, funcIndex FuncIndex, result ValueId, callee ByteArray, arg ValueId, range SourceRange) returns OpIndex
	let args = singletonArgs(arg)
	let opIndex = module.opCount()
	module.ops.push(MaxonOp.call(result, callee: callee, args: args, argLabels: emptyArgLabels(args), argRanges: uniformArgRanges(args, range: range), argImmutableNames: NoImmutableArgNames, argDefaultSlots: NoDefaultArgSlots, mint: CalleeMint.notMinted))
	project.opRanges.record(opIndex, range: range)
	project.valueOrigins.record(funcIndex, valueId: result, opIndex: opIndex)
	return opIndex
end 'appendPromotionRuntimeCall'

// ⭐⭐ **MINT A POST-PARSE SSA VALUE WITHOUT WRITING INTO THE PARSE MEMO.**
//
// `LowerMaxonToStd` reads a value's tag out of `IrFunction.valueTags` for every op it lowers and seeds its
// own numbering from `valueTags.count()`, so a ValueId allocated without an entry in all four dense
// columns would shift every later value's type by one — the invariant `Parser.mintValue` and
// `ModuleInit.SynthesizedFunction.mint` each keep for their own tier.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE FOUR COLUMNS ARE ALIASED WITH THE FILE'S PARSE ARTIFACT.** `IrFunction.shallowCopy` —
// which `IrModule.merge` relocates every function through — carries `valueTags`, `valueNameIds`,
// `valueClosureEnv` and `valueIsCapturingClosure` across BY REFERENCE, and the artifact is memoized:
// `queryAllModule` hands the same object back on a cache hit, and the stdlib body-demand fixpoint re-folds
// artifacts within a single compile. Pushing onto the merged function's column would therefore lengthen
// the ARTIFACT's column, and every later fold would inherit values no op defines. So the first mint
// DE-ALIASES: it replaces each column with a private copy, and only that copy grows. A function this pass
// does not change never mints, so it never copies.
//
// `valueWitnessIds` is deliberately not among them: it is grown on demand and stops at its highest paired
// value, so a ValueId past its end already reads as unpaired — which is what a promoted byte record is.
type PromotedValueMinter
	var func as IrFunction
	var deAliased as bool

	static function over(func IrFunction) returns PromotedValueMinter
		return Self{func: func, deAliased: false}
	end 'over'

	// Allocate a fresh ValueId on the function and record its type, in lockstep. The id IS the column
	// height before the push — the same dense numbering `LowerMaxonToStd` resumes from.
	//
	// The name id is `UnnamedTypeId` for every callee this pass emits, and that is exact rather than a
	// filler: `__str_clone`/`__str_retain` hand back a `String`/`Character` record and `__str_decref`
	// returns `void`, none of which is a `named`/`structRef` value — the only tags whose name column is
	// ever read.
	function mint(tag ValueTypeTag) returns ValueId
		if not self.deAliased 'stillSharedWithTheArtifact'
			self.func.valueTags = self.func.valueTags.clone()
			self.func.valueNameIds = self.func.valueNameIds.clone()
			self.func.valueClosureEnv = self.func.valueClosureEnv.clone()
			self.func.valueIsCapturingClosure = self.func.valueIsCapturingClosure.clone()
			self.deAliased = true
		end 'stillSharedWithTheArtifact'

		let id = self.func.valueTags.count()
		self.func.valueTags.push(tag)
		self.func.valueNameIds.push(UnnamedTypeId)
		self.func.valueClosureEnv.push(0)
		self.func.valueIsCapturingClosure.push(false)
		return id
	end 'mint'
end 'PromotedValueMinter'
